  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Find all values of $n$ for which:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q14a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\phi(n)=4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=5,8,10,12$.\n\nUse prime-power contributions and multiplicativity to exhaust the possibilities.",
          "worked": "Write $n=2^a m$, where $m$ is odd. For each prime power $p^e$ dividing $n$, its contribution $p^{e-1}(p-1)$ must divide $4$. Thus the only possible primes are $2,3,5$; the exponents of $3$ and $5$ are at most $1$. Since including both gives $\\phi(15)=8$, the possible odd parts are $1,3,5$.\n\nIf $m=1$, then $\\phi(2^a)=4$, giving $a=3$ and $n=8$.\n\nIf $m=3$, then $\\phi(2^a)\\phi(3)=4$, so $\\phi(2^a)=2$, giving $a=2$ and $n=12$.\n\nIf $m=5$, then $\\phi(2^a)\\phi(5)=4$, so $\\phi(2^a)=1$, giving $a=0$ or $1$ and $n=5$ or $10$.\n\nTherefore $n=5,8,10,12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q14b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\phi(n)=10$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=11,22$.\n\nRestrict the prime-power contributions to divisors of $10$.",
          "worked": "Every prime divisor $p$ of $n$ satisfies $p-1\\mid10$. The only possible primes are $2,3,11$.\n\nA power of $2$ contributes $1$ or a power of $2$. A positive power of $3$ contributes $2\\cdot3^{e-1}$, so its exponent could only be $1$. Neither can supply the factor $5$ required in $\\phi(n)=10$. Therefore $11$ must divide $n$.\n\nIts exponent is $1$, since a higher exponent contributes a factor $11$. Also $3$ cannot divide $n$, since its contribution would make the product at least $20$.\n\nThus $n=2^a\\cdot11$, with $\\phi(2^a)=1$. Hence $a=0$ or $1$, giving $n=11,22$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q14c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\phi(n)=20$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=25,33,44,50,66$.\n\nUse prime-power contributions to determine every possible odd part.",
          "worked": "Write $n=2^a m$, where $m$ is odd. Every prime divisor $p$ satisfies $p-1\\mid20$, so the only possible primes are $2,3,5,11$. The exponents of $3$ and $11$ are at most $1$, and the exponent of $5$ is at most $2$.\n\nThe factor $5$ in $\\phi(n)$ must come from $5^2$ or $11$.\n\nIf $5^2\\mid n$, its contribution is $\\phi(25)=20$. No other odd prime can occur, and $\\phi(2^a)=1$. This gives $n=25,50$.\n\nIf $11\\mid n$, its contribution is $10$. The prime $5$ cannot occur, since its contribution would make the product at least $40$.\n\nWith odd part $11$, we need $\\phi(2^a)=2$, giving $a=2$ and $n=44$.\n\nWith odd part $3\\cdot11$, the contribution is $2\\cdot10=20$, so $a=0$ or $1$, giving $n=33,66$.\n\nTherefore $n=25,33,44,50,66$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 110
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q14d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\phi(2n)=16$ and $\\phi(3n)=24$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=24,30$.\n\nFirst solve $\\phi(2n)=16$, then test the second condition.",
          "worked": "Put $k=2n$. Each prime divisor $p$ of $k$ satisfies $p-1\\mid16$, so the possible primes are $2,3,5,17$. The odd primes can occur only to the first power.\n\nSince $k$ is even, write $k=2^a m$ with $a\\ge1$. The possible odd parts are $1,3,5,15,17$, with phi values $1,2,4,8,16$, respectively. Combining each with $\\phi(2^a)=2^{a-1}$ gives\n\n$$k=32,48,40,60,34.$$\n\nThus $n=16,24,20,30,17$. Check the second condition:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\phi(3\\cdot16)&=\\phi(48)=16,\\\\\\phi(3\\cdot24)&=\\phi(72)=24,\\\\\\phi(3\\cdot20)&=\\phi(60)=16,\\\\\\phi(3\\cdot30)&=\\phi(90)=24,\\\\\\phi(3\\cdot17)&=\\phi(51)=32.\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore $n=24,30$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      }
    ]
  },
